<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panchatantra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Panchatantra is an ancient Indian collection of animal fables in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, in five treatises, by an unknown author, probably composed around 300 CE. Carried through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a>, Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, it reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and has been called the most frequently translated literary product of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panchatantra.jpg" alt="Panchatantra" width="330" height="514" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Hertel (1915) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panchatantra_page.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gupta-era and medieval texts, among them the Mudrarakshasa, the <b>Panchatantra</b> and Kamandaka's Nitisara, identify Kautilya or Vishnugupta with Chanakya, and the Manu Dharmashastra of the 2nd century CE appears to have used the earlier Dandaniti.</blockquote><a href="/work/arthashastra">Arthashastra · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
